I totally agree with your wish strategy, except for the athame.
Athames are extremely rare, but wands of fire, digging and lightning
are not.  And eventually a Lich will drop an athame.

Poison resistance is vitally important, so once you get a unicorn horn
your first order of business should be to get poison resistance.  Eat
every cave spider (etc) possible until you get one.  Poison res. is
quite simple once you have a unihorn.  A unicorn horn is in fact
arguably the most important thing in the game but it dosn't seem to be
discussed much.

My general rules for wishing are in this priority -

If from a found wand, first wish for 2 scrolls of charging if you have
none yet.  My last ascended game on the tournament I made the mistake
of not wishing for ? of charging first.  I used my first wish on a
magic marker, the wand was used up and I never found a scroll of
charging.  I lost 4 wishes this way.

  1. Silver or Gray armor.  Always the 1st wish in the early game.  If
sokoban has reflection and you can wish for magic resistance you are
75% of the way to winning the game.
  2. A source of reflection or MR, whichever #1 doesn't provide.
  3. Kit armor with a magic side benefit.  That would be:
      - Helm of Brilliance for spellcasters.  You can find a robe
easily.
      - Speed boots - benefits everybody.
      - Gauntlets if you're a fighting class.
  4.  Marker.
  5.  A main weapon, if necessary.  Typically this is a risky wish and
best to get by sacrifice.

Almost all of my wishes are for magic markers, or for a ring of
levitation or slow digestion if I just cannot seem to obtain one
naturally.

My main theory is to obtain items early that provide a benefit every
turn, and that you will use throughout the game.  For instance over the
course of the game a simple pair speed boots gives you twice the
attacks or moves, so the earlier you get them the better.  The earlier
you have reflection the less likely some gnome with a fire wand will
ruin your game.  

